HW-028  The shipping candidate for PR #113311: both commits, on hardware, twice --
        once at the normal block size and once at a block size that forces the DMA
        to chunk. Both clean.

        M5Stack StickS3 (ESP32-S3-PICO-1), Zephyr 4.4.0, 2026-07-13.
        Upstream: issue #113310, PR #113311 (2 commits, head 4cc7a99).

        Supersedes the mutation table in HW-024 and extends HW-027.

WHAT IS UNDER TEST
------------------
Two commits, applied together by scripts/patch_zephyr_dma_quiesce.sh and
scripts/patch_zephyr_i2s_leak.sh:

  1. drivers/dma/dma_esp32_gdma.c -- reset the channel in dma_esp32_stop(), because
     GDMA_INLINK_STOP_CHn is (R/W/SC), a self-clearing command strobe with no
     acknowledge. STOP is a request. The channel goes on writing after it returns.
     HW-027 is the measurement that proves that.

  2. drivers/i2s/i2s_esp32.c -- return the DMA's in-flight mem-slab block, stop the
     RX unit before the DMA on BOTH the GDMA and non-GDMA paths, use dma_stop()'s
     return value, and hand ownership of the block to the receive queue explicitly.

     ESP32 and ESP32-S2 deliberately do NOT free the block: that half is gated behind
     #if SOC_GDMA_SUPPORTED. I cannot run those parts, and the whole subject of this
     series is a stop that looked sufficient and was not.

PROVENANCE OF THE SECOND RUN  (this is the part I got wrong before, so it is written down)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The second run below claims 4096-byte blocks. A log that merely asserts what it is,
is not evidence. So, the chain, each link checkable after the fact:

  source     scripts/../chunk.sh asserted `#define BLOCK_FRAMES 256U` occurred exactly
             once, then replaced it with 1024U. AUDIO_FRAME_BYTES is 4, so
             BLOCK_SIZE went 1024 -> 4096 bytes.

  image      the resulting ELF, read back with nm -S:

                 build-stress   _k_mem_slab_buf_rx_slab   0x2000   = 8 x 1024
                 build-chunk    _k_mem_slab_buf_rx_slab   0x8000   = 8 x 4096

             The block size is baked into the image and can be read out of it. It does
             not depend on trusting the build script.

  flash      that exact build-chunk/zephyr/zephyr.bin was copied to build/chunk.bin and
             written to COM35 with esptool --after watchdog-reset write-flash.

  capture    monitor_capture_windows.py COM35 -> the second log below.

4096 > I2S_ESP32_DMA_BUFFER_MAX_SIZE (4092), so i2s_esp32_rx_callback() takes its
chunking path: chunk 0 = 4092 bytes, chunk 1 = 4 bytes. That is the case where
mem_block is INDEXED (dst = mem_block + chunk_idx * 4092) rather than merely held,
and a 4-byte tail chunk is the nastiest boundary the driver offers.

I checked this after the fact, not before, because I had already written "4096-byte
chunked" into a commit message that was pushed. Twice this week I have reported a
mutation from a build that never contained it. It was in this one. But the habit that
catches that is checking, not remembering.

